  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14108272/assets/5f7c319a0648cdefc0c6a7ba40e6df62a88df92c8bc87fdaadf2afdca46ae0d6.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R,$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $\u2200\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $\u2200\\,y\\in Y,$ $\u2203\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. There are horizontal lines (like the one depicted below) that cross the graph of $f(x)$ more than once. So, $\u2203\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that ${x}_{1}\\ne {x}_{2}$ but $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14108272/assets/bdd421bdd147eb8d0dc9fc593a1205b9a4665bc6d3a3cb76ec7f2e3bf518ecb6.png)\n\n \n\n- Statement II is true. According to the graph, the range of $f(x)$ will cover the entire interval $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement I is false, $f$ is not a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
